Kent Paul Dolan wrote:

> "Richard Bos" <rlb@hoekstra-uitgeverij.nl> wrote:
> 
> [about suggestions to use the Mersene Twister:]
> 
> 
>>Nice, but there are better ones. For example, see
> 
> 
> 
> http://www.google.co.uk/groups?selm=7f7p00dokpnj96inihoise85h0igao3lit%404ax.com
> 
> 
>>in particular the end.
> 
> 
> That one is indeed attractive, the whole program
> in C fits on the screen!
> 
> The unexpressed difficulty in all of these is the
> requirement for "seed integers", though. That RNG
> choice requires one special one and 4096 full 32 bit
> ones.  Few programmers have access to a real source
> of randomness. [Yes, I'm aware there are some
> software tools that use computer system noise as
> sources of randomness, no, I don't know how they
> work or use one. I would expect them to be very
> slow, too, which is not a huge problem if just used
> for seeding a pseudo-random number generator, but is
> worth mentioning.]
> 
> In my own adaption of the (Java) Mersene Twister, I
> seeded the system RNG from the clock, and used it to
> seed the Twister. I don't get a very broad choice of
> starting points in the cycle that way, it is fairly
> easy for another application to have seeded from the
> identical millisecond clock tick, by coincidence,
> and so produce the same string of random integers,
> as used in a parallel application, but once started,
> I get the usual excellent MT behavior. I live with
> the expectation that because the two RNGs are
> separately implemented different algorithms, there
> won't be any unexpectedly nasty consequences of
> using one weak one to seed the other strong one, but
> that's faith/superstiton, not math, talking.
> 
> I could have been smarter by mixing some process ID,
> and perhaps a wordwise Exclusive OR of the files in
> the volitile TEMP directory, into  my starting seed
> for the system RNG, but what I did sufficed for my
> research uses of an RNG in random-hungry search and
> optimization genetic and memetic algorithms.
> 
> There does, also, come a point of diminishing period
> length returns. Once you have enough RNG period to
> describe every baryon and lepton in a randomly
> generated universe in great detail, what do you do
> with the rest of the capability? No conceivable
> computer could run through the full period of one of
> the high performance RNGs in the remaining lifetime
> of the universe.
> 
> As another aside, because of this "one suffices"
> inexhaustable-in-practice RNG period, I modified
> Sean Luke's Java Mersene Twister implementation
> slightly, and made my Mersene Twister a Solitary
> Design Pattern, so I just use one MT for all use
> points in applications, rather than an MT instance
> per user class, synchronizing access on a semaphore,
> something Java makes easy, and which saves me the
> memory footprint of having many instance object MTs
> with large seed array requirements. [I pay a speed
> penalty for the synchronization and added software
> path to the RNG results, though.]
> 
> I also thereby avoid the problem of a computer
> running fast enough that two MT instances are
> created in the span of a single clock tick, seed
> themselves identically, and so spew identical
> random number streams, a disaster in many possible
> RNG usages.
> 
> Similar tricks should work, at least in Java, for
> any pseudo-RNG choice.
> 

A maybe dumb question: Since you seed the MT only
once (and you are also not doing crypto stuff), why do
you need to seed it with hardware randomness (wouldn't
any arbitrary value as seed do just as well)? Thanks.

